Title: Institutional Organization of Innovation Capital Formation: Establishing Organizing Capability for Shared Coordination Infrastructure
Abstract: As innovation systems become increasingly interconnected, the coordination of innovation, capital, markets, and institutional participation progressively depends upon shared coordination infrastructure extending beyond individual organizations or bilateral relationships. The development of these shared institutional environments raises a broader organizational question concerning the organizing capability through which heterogeneous institutions collectively contribute to their governance, stewardship, and long-term continuity. Institutional Organization is introduced as a distinct research domain within Innovation Capital Formation concerned with the institutional conditions supporting this development. Rather than focusing upon the creation of individual institutions or governance arrangements, the analysis examines how diverse institutions collectively participate in the development, governance, stewardship, and continuity of shared coordination infrastructure while preserving their respective mandates, governance responsibilities, and institutional autonomy. Institutional Evolution, Institutional Continuity, and Institutional Architecture are presented as complementary organizational perspectives for understanding this organizing capability. Together, they examine the progressive maturation of shared semantic frameworks, the continuity of institutional participation, and the organizing capability through which institutions collectively contribute to the continuing development of shared coordination infrastructure. These observations position Institutional Organization as an essential institutional dimension of Innovation Capital Formation and provide a conceptual foundation for subsequent research examining the progressive emergence of shared institutional capabilities across existing institutional, financial, regulatory, and economic environments.
